The 45-page declaration has a paragraph on the Iran war. It expresses "deep concern"&#160;over the escalation in the Middle East and calls for “maximum restraint."

That’s BRICS diplomacy —&#160;and the only wording the diverse bloc could agree on. The war directly involves three BRICS members —&#160;Iran, the UAE and Saudi Arabia —&#160;which are on opposite sides of the conflict.&#160;

It took careful diplomacy and night shifts to get even this wording over the finishing line. Still, it is arguably better than nothing —&#160;&#160;especially with all three countries represented in the room, and China offering a mediating role for BRICS.&#160;

That is different from the BRICS declaration in Rio last year, which at least referred to members' "national positions concerning the conflict in Ukraine."

But even then, Russia was not named, its invasion was not condemned and there was no call for Russian troops to withdraw.&#160;

For Putin, the total omission now is politically useful —&#160;especially since he was in Delhi, unlike in Rio.&#160;

Xi Jinping has not visited India since 2019. Relations froze after a deadly clash along their disputed Himalayan border in 2020 .&#160;

His meeting with Modi in Delhi marks another step in a cautious thaw between the two nuclear-armed Asian powers &#160;and perhaps also sends a signal to Washington.&#160;

Both Modi and Xi want a more multipolar world and less Western dominance of the global financial system. But they are coming at it from very different positions.&#160;

China is already the world’s second-largest economy, with a powerful and assertive military. Its focus is on countering Washington.&#160;

India, meanwhile, is pursuing multi-alignment and strategic autonomy &#160;building ties with Russia, the US and the EU for different reasons, without moving into China’s camp.&#160;

Mistrust remains, especially over the contested border and who gets to shape Asia. India’s huge trade deficit with China adds another layer.&#160;

Donald Trump was the elephant in the room. But neither he nor the United States is named in the Delhi declaration.&#160;

They are there nonetheless: BRICS leaders condemn unilateral sanctions and voice concern over unilateral tariffs .&#160;

So there are shared frustrations...
